# XSH casinh(), casinhf() and casinhl()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Only what the page makes exact is tested: special values (signed zeros,
# infinities, NaN parts), the symmetry rules and the exact results it states.
# The accuracy of other results is not tested. Arguments are built with
# CMPLX() and pass through volatile objects, and the three types (name, name
# with f, name with l) are each tested. The special values on this page carry
# the tag for the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point option and are NOTINUSE
# where it is not claimed.

assertion  XSH.casinh.001
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = +0 + i0 gives 0 + i0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/001-zero.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.002
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = x + iInf (x positive finite: 1,
           2.5) gives a result with real part +Inf.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/002-pos-inf-imag.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.003
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = x + iInf (x positive finite: 1,
           2.5) gives imaginary part pi/2.
note       disputed (Q-111): the page names the numbers pi, pi/2, pi/4 and
           3pi/4, which no binary type holds, and does not say the value is
           the nearest representable one. The test expects the decimal value
           rounded to the type. The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559
           Complex Floating-Point option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C
           implementation claims it (Annex G of the C standard). No error is
           defined on this page, so errno is not examined; an exception is
           checked only when math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/003-pos-inf-imag-pi.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.004
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = x + iNaN (x finite, including
           zeros), w = NaN + iy (y non-zero finite) and w = NaN + iNaN give
           NaN + iNaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/004-nan.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.005
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = +Inf + iy (y positive finite: 1,
           2.5) gives +Inf + i0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/005-plus-inf-finite.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.006
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = +Inf + iInf gives a result with
           real part +Inf.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/006-plus-inf-inf.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.007
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = +Inf + iInf gives imaginary part
           pi/4.
note       disputed (Q-111): the page names the numbers pi, pi/2, pi/4 and
           3pi/4, which no binary type holds, and does not say the value is
           the nearest representable one. The test expects the decimal value
           rounded to the type. The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559
           Complex Floating-Point option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C
           implementation claims it (Annex G of the C standard). No error is
           defined on this page, so errno is not examined; an exception is
           checked only when math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/007-plus-inf-inf-pi.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.008
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = +Inf + iNaN gives +Inf + iNaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/008-plus-inf-nan.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.009
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = NaN + i0 gives NaN + i0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/009-nan-zero.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.010
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For casinh, w is the argument. w = NaN + iInf gives an infinite
           real part of either sign and a NaN imaginary part.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT.
test       t/010-nan-inf.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.011
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       casinh of the conjugate of z returns the conjugate of casinh of z,
           part for part (equal values, zero signs included, NaN matching
           NaN), at ordinary points, at real-axis points where branch cuts
           lie, and at points with infinite parts and with a NaN real part.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT. Points with a NaN
           imaginary part are left out (and, for the odd and even rules,
           points with any NaN part): the sign of a NaN carries no required
           meaning. See Q-113.
test       t/011-conjugate-symmetry.c

assertion  XSH.casinh.012
anchor     functions/casinh.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       casinh of -z returns the negation of casinh of z, part for part
           (equal values, zero signs included), at ordinary points, at
           real-axis points where branch cuts lie, and at points with infinite
           parts.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Complex Floating-Point
           option; the test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it
           (Annex G of the C standard). No error is defined on this page, so
           errno is not examined; an exception is checked only when
           math_errhandling includes MATH_ERREXCEPT. Points with a NaN
           imaginary part are left out (and, for the odd and even rules,
           points with any NaN part): the sign of a NaN carries no required
           meaning. See Q-113.
test       t/012-odd-symmetry.c
